[mali-g2] Query commands out of the application's command pools (VORTEK_QUERY_SPLIT=1, prototype, off by default).

The Mali-G2 keeps ~420 bytes for every vkCmdBeginRendering recorded into command pool memory that has
held a query, and keeps it in a cache of the device even after the pool releases, trims or is
destroyed (reused, never given back to malloc): releasing the pool's memory only bounds the peak. With
VORTEK_QUERY_SPLIT=1 the server records vkCmdWriteTimestamp(2), vkCmdResetQueryPool and
vkCmdCopyQueryPoolResults that come before the first or after the last other command of a primary
command buffer into a command buffer of a pool of its own (one per application pool, created on first
use, reset with it, destroyed with it or its device) and submits it right before or after the
application's in vkQueueSubmit / vkQueueSubmit2. Submission order is execution order across the
command buffers of a batch, so the queries mean what they did, and the application's pool never holds
a query. Queries between other commands, inside rendering, begun queries and queries in secondary
command buffers stay (cmd_memory.h's estimate covers them). Device group submits are not expanded
(logged once). Duckov through DXVK 3.1 has only leading timestamps and command buffers of nothing but
timestamps (winlator-0022's statistics on the device). src/query_split.h; tests: frame_leak_test.sh
with VORTEK_QUERY_SPLIT=1 (no release needed: VORTEK_CMD_RELEASE_EVERY=0 stays flat),
FL_QUERY_CHECK=1 (timestamp order), FL_TS_PATTERN (Duckov's pattern).

